Interpreting the scan as requesting the factorial-formula proof of Pascal's identity, for \(1\le k\le n\),
\[
\binom nk+\binom n{k-1}
=\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}+\frac{n!}{(k-1)!(n-k+1)!}
=\frac{(n+1)!}{k!(n+1-k)!}
=\binom{n+1}k.
\]
The boundary cases follow using \(\binom n0=\binom nn=1\).

a) Count pairs \((S,s)\), where \(S\) is a \(k\)-element subset of an \(n\)-element set and \(s\in S\). Choosing \(S\) first gives \(k\binom nk\). Choosing the distinguished element \(s\) first and then the other \(k-1\) elements gives \(n\binom{n-1}{k-1}\). Hence the two expressions are equal.

b) Algebraically,
\[
k\binom nk=k\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}
=\frac{n!}{(k-1)!(n-k)!}
=n\frac{(n-1)!}{(k-1)!(n-k)!}
=n\binom{n-1}{k-1}.
\]
